1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,740
This park ranger was struck by
lightning seven times,

2
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:04,430
and survived every single one.

3
00:00:04,708 --> 00:00:07,858
A lightning bolt is about five
times hotter than the surface of

4
00:00:07,868 --> 00:00:08,448
the sun.

5
00:00:08,458 --> 00:00:09,298
So how did he survive?

6
00:00:09,667 --> 00:00:11,967
Often, most of the current
doesn't go through your body.

7
00:00:12,207 --> 00:00:13,887
It races over your wet skin
instead.

8
00:00:14,333 --> 00:00:15,293
That's called a flashover.

9
00:00:15,542 --> 00:00:17,602
But the current that gets inside
can stop your heart.

10
00:00:17,833 --> 00:00:20,693
Sometimes it restarts on its
own, but breathing often
doesn't.

11
00:00:20,853 --> 00:00:22,953
That's why fast CPR saves lives.

12
00:00:23,417 --> 00:00:26,407
Survivors can also be left with
these strange red ferns on their

13
00:00:26,417 --> 00:00:27,027
skin,

14
00:00:27,037 --> 00:00:28,137
called Lichtenberg figures.

15
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:30,880
They're not burns, and they fade
within a day or two.

16
00:00:31,208 --> 00:00:33,828
About nine in ten people struck
by lightning survive.

17
00:00:34,292 --> 00:00:36,692
But only one man has ever
survived it seven times.

18
00:00:37,125 --> 00:00:37,925
Roy Sullivan.

19
00:00:38,005 --> 00:00:39,005
The human lightning rod.
